Z||000000||Bruce||11-23-2000||01:07 PM||||Is it just me, or is the fan switch pretty difficult to get to and remove. I just didn't see any way other than to start removing surrounding stuff, but I'm not going to do that and screw up anything else.<BR>Happy Thanksgiving!<BR>bb||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rodney||11-23-2000||09:42 PM||||Squeeze the wide sides of the connector and it comes right off. You have to use a plyers that gets down in there. Use a socket and extension. If the engine is cold you will loose no coolant. Easy to do. All things considered it is an easy job. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bruce||11-24-2000||02:10 PM||||Sorry, but it's not the "easy" process you say. It's too cramped and too easy to break things in that area.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fieroboy1||11-24-2000||06:02 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Bruce:<BR>[B]Is it just me, or is the fan switch pretty difficult to get to and remove. <P>It's just you. j/k.  You should be able to reach in with pliers to pull the connector off.  After that it's just a matter of putting a socket on the sensor and removing it.<p>[This message has been edited by Fieroboy1 (edited 11-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||11-25-2000||10:02 AM||||It's one of those things that you swear at for a while, and root through your entire tool box for the right tool to reach it with, but in the end it takes about 20 min. to get it out and about 45 seconds to replace.<P>My old switch seemed to be plastic surrounded by a metal shell and of course it broke and required a pair of vice grips to remove. Rodney's new switch seems to be a pretty stout brass casting...installed easily with the right size socket.||||reg||1
